00:02:21.111 --> 00:02:23.410
YONIT GRUBER-HAZANI:
Thanks, Amir.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.300
Hi, everyone.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.675
I'm Yonit, and I've been a
Wazer for the last two years.

00:02:28.675 --> 00:02:32.820
I'm a DevOps on Waze
infrastructure team.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.450
And let's see a show of hands
if you used a navigation app

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.992
this week.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:39.950
We-- of course you did.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:40.580
We all do.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.160
I do it-- use it daily.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.650
So for the people
who don't know Waze,

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.350
Waze is crowdsourced
navigation app.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.550
It's free.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:55.070
And among our best features
are timesaving routes and maps

00:02:55.070 --> 00:03:01.670
that are updated daily thanks to
our 500,000 wonderful volunteer

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:07.390
map editors who updates
the maps all the time.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:13.240
So we currently have around 100
million active users monthly.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.900
This size of infrastructure--

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.560
this size of a amount of users
takes a huge infrastructure

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.790
to support it.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.720
So we have thousands of
instances running on hundreds

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.730
of autoscaling groups.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.660
We have about 2 petabytes
of cassandra data running

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.140
on 1,000 cassandra
instances, which makes us

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.001
one of the biggest deployments
of cassandra in the world

00:03:37.001 --> 00:03:37.500
today.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.900
This is the amount
of API calls running

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.410
through our servers between
the different microservices.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:51.440
This is a lot of activity, and
it needs a lot of monitoring.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.990
So we monitor things that
show us the service level

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:57.680
that we give to our users.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.680
So we monitor the
amount of traffic.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.570
That's the query per minute.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.630
We monitor the performance
of the service.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:04.870
That's the latency.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.250
And we monitor, of
course, failure rates.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.250
So when you have
such a big system

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.040
and you help people
with their driving,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.149
downtime is not an option.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:21.829
We-- when an online game is
down, people are annoyed.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.620
I know when my boy's--

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:27.650
my son's online game is
down, he's devastated.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:30.960
But when Waze is
down, people get lost.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:38.120
So in some countries when
Waze is down, within minutes

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.190
it's in the news--

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:40.610
in the national news.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.290
That's how essential we are.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:45.890
So try to imagine that you are
a DevOps on the infrastructure

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:46.570
team.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.250
You're trying to debug
a problem at rush hour.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.530
Everything is
failing, and reporters

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:54.350
are calling you to ask
details about what's going on.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.080
That's a very
stressing situation,

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.600
and we've been there.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.470
So downtime is not an
option, but downtime happens.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.540
And I use Waze everyday.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.820
When I drive home, I want
to know how much time it's

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.680
going to take me to
get to the school

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.500
to pick up my kids so I
can call ahead and beg

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.140
for mercy from the teacher.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:20.370
So we feel responsible to keep
our system online all the time.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.060
So now that you understand
the size of the challenge

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.520
and what we need to
monitor and how big

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.130
it is, let's take a
look at the technology

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.930
stacks that actually
we use at Waze

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:35.190
so you can see how we carry
this amount of traffic.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.270
Waze is built on microservices.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:43.030
We use Java-based code.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.040
We run on standard
instances that are

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.080
part of an autoscaling group.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.410
We use redis or memcache
as a caching layer,

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:54.730
and we use cassandra or
Postgres as the databases.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:56.960
Now most of our services
are pretty similar.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.130
They'll look the same, but
we have a few outliers.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.740
Some are not to Java based,
and some are not even

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.210
running on instances.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:10.570
We use as well Kubernetes and
App Engine and Cloud Functions.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.960
Now these hundreds
of microservices,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.890
they are talking to each other.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.160
Sometimes they're servers,
and sometimes they're clients.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.330
But most of the time, they are
both, passing data between them

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:23.570
with API calls.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:28.900
So to do that, we use propriety
communication protocol.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.660
And this protocol also
collects statistics

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.480
about these API calls between
the different microservices

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:36.880
and sends them into
a central location.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.730
So we have a place to
pick up all the statistics

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:42.035
about the performance
and usage of these APIs.

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:47.660
Our production critical
servers are also

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.180
split into dozens of
geographical shards.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:51.080
Now why is that?

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.640
Because it helps
to spread the load

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.100
between the different
areas, and it

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.100
reduces the impact of bad
updates when we deploy

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.710
code into production.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.650
Now seems pretty
complicated, doesn't it?

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.480
Well, you haven't
heard the half of it.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.340
This is only on one of
our logical data centers--

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:14.470
bless you-- split
across three regions.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.340
And that was only
the front end parts--

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.500
the online part, which has
the actual user experience.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.390
We also have an
offline part, which

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.640
is that-- which is doing
the machine learning and map

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:25.720
building.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.600
And we use big data tools
to do the post-processing.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.000
We use MapReduce and
Athena and everything

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.500
needs to be monitored.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:34.000
Oh!

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.930
And double everything
I just said because we

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.810
use both GCP and AWS.

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:41.480
To support deployments to
these two different clouds,

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.020
we are using Spinnaker open
source to deploy our code.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.780
This gives us an obstruction
layer between the clouds

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.190
and allow for seamless
management of GCP and AWS.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.380
So what we see here
in this specific slide

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.620
is a sample of a microservice
that does routing.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:03.000
You can see on the right hand
that we have 30s and more API

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.290
calls that it's using to talk
to about five microservices.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:10.350
And on the left side,
you can see the scale--

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.240
you can see this is--
all this is in one

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.980
of these rectangle on the left.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.730
So you can see the size and
the amount of the API calls.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.940
This is what brings us to
this staggering number of 25

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.980
million API calls per minute.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.860
On top of monitoring such a
large-scale infrastructure,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:32.179
there are other things to
consider when monitoring.

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:33.890
We have new features.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.710
We have hundreds of engineers
working all the time

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:39.380
to extend our system,
add new features,

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.289
and deploy new microservices
into production.

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:45.020
These needs to be added
with monitoring on the fly

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:49.160
without interruption and with
zero config from the developer

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.070
side.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:52.780
There are hardware failures.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.570
Imagine we have thousands
of disks running around

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:56.370
in the system.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.690
And as you all know, disks fail.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.040
This is something that they do.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.420
So we need to
monitor that as well.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:04.500
We have quotas.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.600
When you use a cloud
service, you always

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:11.760
get to the 100% of quota
usage on any given API at 3:00

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.410
AM in the morning usually.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.830
So we want to alert on
the 75% of the quota

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.740
before we get there so we have
time to increase it and request

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:24.240
for more because you all
know that takes time as well.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.400
And certificates and
licenses are also

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.980
something that expire within
the large infrastructure.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:32.070
So we want to be alerted
30 days in advance

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.730
so we can request for extension
or replace those certificates

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.140
with a new one.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.510
And, of course, service SLO.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:41.990
Every service that we monitor.

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:43.640
We want to know
the quality of it,

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:48.010
so we need to know UPM, that's
the query per minute, failure

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.550
rate and latency
for the performance.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:59.460
And, of course, don't
forget about the scaling.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:02.190
And this whole thing
is very dynamic.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:05.020
Imagine that we have the
geographical chartings,

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:06.780
and each country has
a different driving

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.850
hours and driving habits.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:10.380
And you have holidays.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:15.300
Any autoscaling group can grow
from three servers up to 120

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.750
throughout the day and
shrink back at night.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.470
Well, you can't
monitor something

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.210
like that with
configuration files.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.150
So now that you know
how complicated it is

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.580
and what are the
challenges, can you

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:31.430
imagine how to start deploying
a monitoring service on such

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:33.800
a large-scale system?

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.510
Ladies and gentlemen,
I present to you

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.850
the man behind the machine.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:46.400
He who brought the order out of
chaos, the Python ninja, Evgeny

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.581
Andzhelo.

00:10:48.581 --> 00:10:51.383
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:52.714 --> 00:10:54.130
EVGENY ANDZHELO:
Thank you, Yonit.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:55.780
Hi, everybody.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.990
I'm Evgeny, and I'm
going to show you

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.980
how Stackdriver makes sense
of our complicated system

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.910
that Yonit just told you about.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:07.670
Firstly, it wasn't
stable enough, and we--

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:10.900
it took us a while to
find out when it was down.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:14.680
Secondly, monitoring new
microservices in our system

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.040
was complicated
and time consuming,

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.220
which was a big problem
because new microservices were

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:25.170
being added constantly, and we
were having trouble keeping up.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:28.320
About two years ago, we heard
about new monitoring system

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.780
called Stackdriver.

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:34.220
Stackdriver offers
powerful tools

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:37.280
for collecting and using
time series metrics.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.950
We thought it was pretty
cool because it was easier

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:44.420
to see the status of an issue
over a long period of time.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.940
Stackdriver automatically
collects metrics

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:50.180
from our GCP instances
and services.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.680
It can also import the
data from AWS CloudWatch

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.660
with just a few
clicks of a button.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:58.480
Thanks to this, we
hit the ground running

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:03.680
and got tons of useful info
without any effort on our part.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.100
The built-in metrics
were great, but we also

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:10.970
needed another type of
metrics, and Stackdriver

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:13.340
allowed us to send
custom metrics

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:15.830
with all info we needed.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:19.430
It's a really flexible system
with great functionality.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:23.300
We also found out that using
their API was super easy,

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.310
and the system was very stable.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:29.840
And it could process a large
amount of metrics quickly.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:33.190
We realized that with
Stackdriver, the sky

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.190
was the limit, and we
started adding new metrics

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:38.980
from all the various
corners of our system.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:42.460
For example, we started writing
metrics like request rate

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:45.810
and latency and gave them
labels according to their cloud

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.250
platform, region, and more.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:51.700
So that way you could see
in one metric information

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:55.620
from instances running
on both AWS and GCP.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.960
After we added all this
data into Stackdriver,

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.300
we started thinking about
how to put it to good use.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.000
First of all, we
wanted to make it

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:10.260
more easily accessible for
us and for our developers.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.170
And we did that by
using dashboard API

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.980
to create specific dashboards
for all our critical services.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.350
They basically
allowed us to display

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:24.060
a lot of useful information
both building and custom

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.540
for even any given service
in a single pane of glass.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:33.950
Second, we wanted to be alerted
when specific metrics meet

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:35.750
pretty fine conditions.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:39.570
To accomplish that, we started
using alerting API to create

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:41.990
alert policies, which
basically allowed

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:45.980
us to define one or more
conditions that will trigger

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:49.760
an alert and define how the
alert will be delivered using

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:53.700
notification channels like
webhooks that can report a bug,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:57.590
page their own code, or trigger
our autohealing actions,

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.470
which allow our internal
scripts to try and fix

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:02.600
the issue before we
wake up the on-call.

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:08.190
Over time, we edit
even more metrics

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:12.330
such as failure rate, cache and
database layer state, errors,

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.110
and [INAUDIBLE]
client side metrics.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:18.710
We could also use the data that
already existed in Stackdriver

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:22.390
to compare our current state
to the state we had last week

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:25.380
or at some other
time in the past.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:29.100
This helped us find anomalies
and other irregular activity,

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.870
which had previously
been next to impossible.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.450
For example, let's
assume that we usually

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:38.740
have about 10,000 logins
per minute in the Bay Area

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:40.420
during peak hour.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:44.500
And today that number
is 10 times higher.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:47.050
This could point to a real
problem like a [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:49.480
storm, and we need to
be able to see that.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.880
Now let's talk about automation.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:57.620
After a period of
using Stackdriver,

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:00.390
we thought that a lot of
our service dashboards

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:02.070
were very similar.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:05.310
So we thought why not
automate their creation.

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:08.520
And we did that using
a schedule process that

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.950
scans for new
services in our system

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.440
and automatically creates a
dashboard using pretty fun

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:18.870
templates without any additional
work for us or the developers.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:22.190
You have no idea how
much time it saved us.

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:24.350
It also reduced
the time it takes

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:27.020
us to find the root
cause of issues

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:30.380
and resolve them because all
the dashboards are created

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:33.705
using the same template.

00:15:33.705 --> 00:15:37.460
The scheduled process also
create alerts for news services

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:40.640
with default threshold
and notification channels.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:44.090
After a loading period,
we adjust them so then

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:48.850
they actually notify the on-call
when there are any issues.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.190
Both dashboard and
policy definitions,

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.530
whether manually or
automatically created,

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:55.690
are saved in the
GIT report and can

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:58.390
be modified by the developers.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:01.480
And any developer
working in new service

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:03.910
can add themselves as
notification channel

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:09.000
and debug the issue before the
service moves to production.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.420
Everything we just talked about
helped our team sleep better at

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:16.770
night with less false positive
and resolve issue faster.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.370
Now I'd like to show
you a demo based

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.680
on an issue we had
in the past that

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:28.324
was solved quickly and
easily thanks to Stackdriver.

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:36.850
So let's say that I'm
currently on call.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:39.180
It's the middle of
the night, and I just

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:42.230
got a page on my phone.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:45.230
I can see that it's a page
about high failure rate for one

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:47.120
of our routing services.

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:51.500
So I wake up, open my laptop
to see more information

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:52.439
about the alert.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.405
In the email we can see
a link to open incidents

00:17:00.405 --> 00:17:04.400
to our playbook and the
link to the dashboard.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:07.170
In the bottom, we can
also see the resources

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:10.200
that triggered the alert.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.079
Before I move onto
the dashboard,

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:15.599
let's look at the
configuration of this alert.

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:24.119
We can see that it's
pretty easy to read,

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.440
and the developer
can easily modify

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.050
the notification channel's
duration or threshold

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:30.400
if needed.

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:34.540
The combiner allows me to
choose whether to trigger

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.720
the alert if all the
conditions are met

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.682
or at least one of them.

00:17:39.682 --> 00:17:45.080
In the notification field,
we can choose one more.

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:47.400
For example, we can
see-- in the example,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:51.520
we can see that the alert
will send-- enable end trigger

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:57.350
our webhook that we'll
call autohealing bot

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:58.870
with the details
of the incident.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:05.970
This is a dashboard that
was linked in the email.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.930
The problem can be seen in the
server API failure at widget.

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.820
Now I'm freed of the
metrics in the widget

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:14.440
to focus on the specific
metrics related to the issue.

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:21.610
After scrolling down a
bit in the dashboard,

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:24.250
I see something interesting
in another widget

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:28.210
displaying service
dependencies failure rate.

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:31.230
And by dependencies, I
mean all the services

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:34.220
that routing needs
to function properly.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:37.540
I see that geocoding
has a high failure rate.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:39.890
It started at the
exact same time.

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:42.070
So I go to the
geocoding dashboard

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:44.560
and see something weird in
the cassandra latency widget.

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:49.720
Read latency peaked just before
our routing issue started, so I

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.760
connected to our
cassandra cluster

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:55.770
and manually fix the issue
using our cassandra playbook.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:57.840
In the future, this
issue won't even

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.870
wake me up because we are
very close to finishing

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:03.360
our cassandra
autohealing process that

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.780
will fix it automatically.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:09.750
So as I just
demonstrated, Stackdriver

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:12.030
gives great visibility
into our system

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:15.240
and helps us resolve issues
much faster than before.

00:19:18.649 --> 00:19:20.597
YONIT GRUBER-HAZANI:
Thanks, Evgeny.

00:19:20.597 --> 00:19:23.519
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:29.640
So we have hundreds of
engineers working all the time

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.790
on improving the system,
adding new microservices,

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:35.220
and extending the existing ones.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:37.170
Before we had all
these automation tools

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.570
that Evgeny just spoke
about, all of this

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:41.970
was done manually
in the DevOps team.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:45.600
So at some time in
the progress, we just

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:47.550
couldn't keep up
with the workload.

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:51.330
And large parts of the system
were inadequately monitored,

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:55.060
and some parts were
not monitored at all.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:56.760
So let's take a look
how it looks now

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.660
with all the
automation in place.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:02.100
Let's say that I'm
a developer at Waze,

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.000
and I just wrote my
new cool microservice

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.280
and deployed it into
the staging area.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.070
Now I used the Java
microservice framework

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.170
that all the
developers use in Waze.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:15.110
And this framework includes
our monitoring modules

00:20:15.110 --> 00:20:18.950
already written, and it will
start working automatically.

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:21.230
Those modules-- I'll tell
you a little bit more

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:22.940
about these modules.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:25.280
As you can see
from the beginning,

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:28.430
this will-- dashboard
will be auto-generated and

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:32.150
auto-deployed into Stackdriver
with the Stackdriver dashboard

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:35.990
API as soon as I
deployed my service.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:39.470
And I'll show you each of these
cubes because it's very little.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:43.670
So the first thing that we'll
see are the server APIs.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:45.320
These are the same
APIs that I've

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.300
been talking before for
the specific service

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:49.160
that I'm looking at.

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:51.440
And I will see query
per minute and I'll

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.310
see server failure rate and
I'll see also the latency.

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:59.180
You can see on the right side
in the right upper cube also

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:01.020
server diff for the API.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:03.314
This is the QPM from last week.

00:21:03.314 --> 00:21:04.730
So we can see a
comparison and see

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:08.690
if there is a change in the
behavior of the application.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.230
The next set of widgets
are the server exceptions.

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:15.130
Now this is from
the application log.

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:17.800
We collect those
included modules inside

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:19.180
of the Java framework.

00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:21.430
We include all the
errors from the log,

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:24.580
and we export them as a
graph in the Stackdriver.

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:28.210
So I can see from
the same dashboard

00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:30.620
how the application
is performing.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:33.610
Now the client APIs is like
what you Evgeny said before.

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:36.590
These are the clients that are
connecting to my microservice.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:38.950
So I can see as well how
they are performing also

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:41.050
in the same dashboard
for the microservice.

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:44.260
So I can see how my service
failure is affecting

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.750
other service in Waze.

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:49.630
And the last set here
are the dependencies.

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:53.410
So my server is connecting
to other microservices.

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:56.410
And we can see here the
QPM, the Query Per Minute.

00:21:56.410 --> 00:22:01.000
We can see the timeouts,
and we can see the errors

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:03.880
for connecting to
other services.

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:07.600
So I can see from this dashboard
if the problem is actually

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:09.850
in my microservice or
maybe its downstream

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:11.080
in a different microservice.

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.990
So I can continue going
between the microservices

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:16.480
until I get to the problem one.

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:17.830
Also we can see here--

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:20.350
from those included
monitoring modules--

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:22.960
we can see that the memcache
QPM, query per minute,

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:24.100
is showing up.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:26.110
All the data layer
is also exposed

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:29.320
in the same dashboard, so I can
see if the specific service is

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:32.280
having a problem with a
memcache or with a cassandra

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:33.490
it's connecting to.

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:38.000
I can even see which table
is eating up all the space.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:41.170
So these metrics are
great, but they're not

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:43.000
enough for my
particular use case.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:47.080
Let's say that my microservices
is also writing and reading

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:48.190
from a queue.

00:22:48.190 --> 00:22:51.340
So I want to see how-- what
is the QPM of writing messages

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:52.190
to the queue.

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:55.870
Now my application already
exports these metrics,

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:57.430
and there are already
in Stackdriver

00:22:57.430 --> 00:22:58.960
thanks to the included modules.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:02.060
But I want to see it on
the default dashboard.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:04.960
So what I need to do is
edit one configuration

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:08.240
file from our Stackdriver
configuration repo,

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:12.100
which is saved in GIT, push it
into production, and that's it.

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:14.620
In a couple of minutes,
Evgeny's automation tool

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:16.540
will pick up on
the change in GIT,

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:20.800
and we'll add that widget via
the Stackdriver dashboard APIs

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:24.010
into the dashboard
that I'm using.

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:26.950
As a developer, I can also
open a new config file

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:29.950
and create a whole new
dashboard only with the metrics

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:34.380
that I want to see when
I'm debugging a problem.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:38.000
All these changes and
repeating dashboard structure

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:41.570
helps me at 3:00 AM to
solve production issues.

00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:44.090
I can tell which table in
the cassandra is failing.

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:46.430
I can see exactly
what the problem

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:48.440
is from the logs in
the same dashboard.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.815
So the problem-- so instead
of just getting an alert Waze

00:23:51.815 --> 00:23:57.080
is down or the website is down,
I get an alert saying service a

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:58.970
is failing, and
in the dashboard,

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:01.250
I can see that it's
failing because of service

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:03.830
b, which is downstream
a dependency that

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:05.300
is not performing well.

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:10.290
Looking into the future, we
are using Spinnaker open source

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.190
to deploy our services.

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:14.820
So now we are starting
to integrate the metrics

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:17.130
from Stackdriver
into the Kayenta

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:19.410
tool, the new Kayenta
from Spinnaker,

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:21.760
that will take these metrics.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:25.980
And when you're doing a deploy,
it will fire up a few instances

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:28.230
and do a comparison
of the metrics

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:31.710
between the new instances and
the older autoscaling group

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:35.070
to decide if to move forward
with their deployment

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:38.190
or to stop and kill those
instances and rollback.

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:40.200
So this way the
deployment process

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:42.630
is automatically
deciding if to continue,

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:45.970
and no human
intervention is needed.

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:47.830
This, of course,
will reduce downtime

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:50.910
if a bad deploy is done.

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:54.620
So this is me and my league
of extraordinary gentlemen.

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:58.220
We are mostly DevOps
and a few developers.

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:01.340
And this tiny DevOps team
handles this whole operation

00:25:01.340 --> 00:25:02.720
in its up time.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:06.500
We have one on call
for primary issues

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:10.460
and one person who wakes up
at night and one secondary

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:13.340
to handle the bugs that the
star driver and a notification

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:16.190
channel can open.

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:17.900
And where are we today?

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:21.410
We are sending 2 and 1/2
million time series per minute

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:23.360
into Stackdriver.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:27.110
We have thousands of dashboards
all created automatically

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:32.640
through Stackdriver and
are created automatically

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:35.250
through Stackdriver
dashboard API.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:38.250
We have hundreds of
policies all set up

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:40.480
on the critical time series.

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:43.480
So we know when
something is failing.

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:48.300
And now I'll hand you back to
Amir, who will put everything

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:51.870
into perspective.

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:54.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:56.950
AMIR HERMELIN: Thank
you very much, Yonit.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:57.700
Thank you, Evgeny.

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:07.730
So the next time you use
