00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:17.360
Now you know the story.

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:18.540
This is the team.

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:19.920
Other members are lined up.

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:22.430
Also, if you can recognize
them from the photos.

00:26:22.430 --> 00:26:25.929
So that's the story of how--

00:26:25.929 --> 00:26:27.470
the approach they've
taken, but let's

00:26:27.470 --> 00:26:29.690
talk a little bit
about Stackdriver,

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:35.030
which is the platform and
collection of services

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:38.790
that they use to enable
this monitoring approach.

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:42.350
So first of all, what
Yonit and Evgeny described

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:45.560
as how they use Stackdriver,
we've focused mostly

00:26:45.560 --> 00:26:46.490
around monitoring.

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:49.130
So Stackdriver is
a platform that

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:54.710
enables you to easily collect
metrics, logs, and other data

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:57.260
into a central repository.

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:00.890
They take that from
open source components,

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:07.640
from applications that are
running in Google Cloud or AWS.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:13.910
We use APIs and other
methods to ingest.

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:17.270
Yonit did mention the
dashboard API, which

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:18.860
is currently in early access.

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:20.670
We intend to release
it in the future,

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:22.970
but it's not available
to everyone right now.

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.730
But the alerting API
as well as other APIs

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:27.830
mentioned is available.

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:29.980
And then you take that
data-- first of all,

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:33.680
Stackdriver enables you to store
it to maintain it for longer

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:37.190
periods and to
create visualizations

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:39.110
and insights from
it, visualizations

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:42.260
in the form of dashboards.

00:27:42.260 --> 00:27:47.090
Insights are sometimes
automatically inferred

00:27:47.090 --> 00:27:49.490
from the data and
the correlation

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:54.680
as well as generate alerts
via our various notification

00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:55.670
channels.

00:27:55.670 --> 00:28:00.230
So Evgeny mentioned
email and webhook.

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:05.610
We support other channels such
as PagerDuty, Slack, SMS, et

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:06.110
cetera.

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:08.850
And we are expanding
our support for that.

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:11.300
And finally Stackdriver
also supports

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:13.590
other third-party
vendor ecosystems,

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.410
so you can integrate
with other tools that

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:18.650
can use the theta
and other services

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:23.270
in the cloud, one example
being taking logging data

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:26.120
and automatically
ingesting it to BigQuery

00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:28.870
for deeper analysis.

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:32.260
Just to complete the picture,
Stackdriver is not only

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:34.090
about these capabilities.

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:35.920
We're building more.

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:38.820
So we're building
context capabilities

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:41.570
and autoinferring of services.

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:47.590
We're expanding more of our
visualization technologies,

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:51.610
adding tools that can
help you better mitigate

00:28:51.610 --> 00:28:55.880
issues and deal with incident
response, et cetera, et cetera.

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:59.350
And you can hear about it
in other lectures as well.

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:04.180
And just going over to
the different Stackdriver

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.600
products available to you.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:08.110
So we mainly touched
upon monitoring,

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:11.890
which you can use to visualize
an alert on the health

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:13.690
of your application.

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:16.030
Stackdriver also has
a logging product

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:19.030
that collects your
logs in some cases

00:29:19.030 --> 00:29:20.980
automatically depending
on the service

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:27.820
and lets you analyze
them and do fast queries

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:31.390
and even do things
like create metrics

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:34.300
from logs so you can use
those metrics in dashboards

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:38.320
and alerting policies and
other advanced capabilities.

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:40.050
We also have error
reporting, which

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:43.140
can automatically group
and detect and alert you

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:46.410
on exceptions and
failures in your code.

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:48.350
And, again, the nice
thing is that this suite

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:51.600
is tightly integrated, so
let's say you already are--

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:54.270
you already have
logs in the platform,

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:56.490
then error reporting
comes from free.

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:59.340
You don't need to
instrument another service.

00:29:59.340 --> 00:30:03.360
You don't need to sign
up for something else.

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:06.010
It's automatically
available for you.

00:30:06.010 --> 00:30:09.930
We also have trace with
distributed trace, which

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:15.060
we support via API on the
SDK, Zipkin collectors,

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:17.000
and it automatically
collects strays

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:20.310
and analyzes your
latency for applications

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:23.200
running in App Engine.

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:26.200
Debug is another
very, very useful tool

00:30:26.200 --> 00:30:30.290
that lets you debug
applications in production.

00:30:30.290 --> 00:30:32.500
So you can-- during
production, you

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:35.710
can insert breakpoints
and logging statements

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:37.094
without all the overhead.

00:30:37.094 --> 00:30:39.010
So you don't need to
instrument it in advance.

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:40.960
It's a really cool
capability, and I

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:46.810
hope you can see a demo of it
in one of our sessions here.

00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:50.050
And finally profiler,
which is an--

00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:52.380
it's actually in beta.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:56.380
And so it's available, and it
helps you pinpoint hotspots

00:30:56.380 --> 00:30:58.300
in your running--

00:30:58.300 --> 00:31:02.240
in our applications
running in the cloud.

00:31:02.240 --> 00:31:05.840
So to recap it,
firstly how many of you

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:08.300
are running services
that you believe

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:10.400
are larger scale than Waze?

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:15.195
Nice.

00:31:15.195 --> 00:31:15.820
Which services?

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:19.000
Spotify.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:20.166
Yeah, that's a huge service.

00:31:20.166 --> 00:31:22.330
That's great.

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:22.830
Love it.

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:26.320
I was hoping at least one
person would raise your hand.

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:29.010
But for the rest of you,
this also applies to you.

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:30.990
This approach can
also apply to you.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.900
So let's recap what
we talked about today.

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:37.710
We talked about how
large the service is

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:41.580
and how challenging
it is to monitor it,

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:46.050
and what are the implications of
not catching errors real time,

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.770
what it means to users.

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:50.430
In the case of Waze,
it can mean people

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:54.240
not reaching their
destinations on time or at all,

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:56.280
or it can be even--

00:31:56.280 --> 00:32:00.280
sometimes it can even make it
to the news in some countries.

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:02.010
So downtime is
definitely user pain

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:07.590
and the team that is
tasked with keeping

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:10.230
the application up--
it's not a huge team,

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:12.510
so they need an approach.

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:15.720
The system is very dynamic,
which creates more challenges.

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:19.620
So any developer can
add microservices,

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:21.480
remove microservices on the fly.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:24.190
They don't need to
check in with this team.

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:26.370
And it could be developers
in different floors

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:27.797
or in different buildings.

00:32:27.797 --> 00:32:29.880
So the approach the team
has taken is first of all

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:33.030
to decide which metrics--

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:35.400
which signals they
collect, what they

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.310
look at in a more canonical way.

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:41.760
For all the microservices,
what are we interested in?

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:43.120
What metrics?

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:44.490
Which dashboards?

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:46.150
What alerting policies?

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:47.850
They started there.

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:52.770
After that, they fine tuned
because different microservices

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:54.360
have different requirements.

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:57.120
So you can build and customize.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:01.410
And when you have that canonical
approach across the board built

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:03.870
in as much automation
as possible,

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:08.760
so the developers use config
files and the alerting policies

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:11.820
and the dashboards and the
metrics that are adjusted.

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:15.600
Everything is automatically
generated to reduce human error

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:19.230
and also to promote unity.

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:22.380
So when problems
occur, engineers

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:25.380
can look at different
microservices

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:29.130
in the same manner, a
similar pane of glass.

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:30.540
So automation is a key part.

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:34.620
And lastly we talked about
the specific monitoring

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:39.030
suite of products that Waze
uses, which is Stackdriver.

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:41.400
Stackdriver is
available free to try.

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:46.370
Just google Stackdriver
and get started.

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:48.290
Just lastly other talks--

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:50.530
I'm not going to
go over each one,

00:33:50.530 --> 00:33:54.400
but these are talks
related to monitoring,

00:33:54.400 --> 00:34:00.540
to SRE best practices, to
how Waze and Spinnaker--

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:03.110
Waze uses Spinnaker together.

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:04.540
These are all great talks.

00:34:04.540 --> 00:34:07.810
We recommend that
you attend them

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:10.090
either today or the next days.

00:34:10.090 --> 00:34:13.090
And don't forget to
rate our session,

00:34:13.090 --> 00:34:14.290
especially if you liked it.

